{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Delete `baseline_advanced_at` and stop rewriting an unchanged baseline. This is the single\nhighest-value change in the epic: it cuts marginal packed cost per `bindings.json` version from\n**12.79 KB to 1.74 KB (7.4x)**, measured over 40 consecutive real revisions. session_log **1cb6-d553-6565-49b4** (measured facts; do not re-derive).\n\n## Why\n`binding_store.py:373-375` unconditionally rewrites `entry[\"baseline\"]` and stamps\n`baseline_advanced_at = _now_iso()`. That field is written in exactly one place and **read nowhere in\n`src/`** (only asserted in `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_binding_baseline.py:61`). Measured\nacross consecutive committed revisions: baseline VALUES changed **0** entries while the stamp changed\n**1144** — a scattered edit across a sort-ordered 18 MB file, which is pathological for git delta\ncompression.\n\nHonest scoping: because baseline values change 0 entries/pass today, the write-when-changed guard is\n**byte-neutral** and the entire measured win comes from deleting the field. The guard is still worth\nadding — it is the correct behaviour once real Jira edits arrive, and it saves the rewrite CPU — but\nit must not be credited with the savings.\n\n## Approach\nIn `set_baseline`: build the filtered baseline, compare to the existing one, return early if equal;\nnever write `baseline_advanced_at`. Preserve `binding_store.py:374`'s `if k in fields` semantics\n(**not** `is not None`) — `outbound_field_diff.py:251` treats a MISSING key as \"no ancestor -> local\nwins\", so a key must stay present with a `None` value rather than being dropped.\n\nBoth callers must be covered: `reconcile._advance_baselines` (`reconcile.py:506-523`) and the\ninbound-adopt path at `apply_inbound_records.py:209-212`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Two consecutive reconciler passes over unchanged Jira state produce a **byte-identical**\n      `bindings.json` and create **no commit**. Tier: **integration** against a stateful fake,\n      asserting file bytes and commit count — not call counts. (`save()` uses\n      `json.dump(indent=2, sort_keys=True)`, so byte-identity is well-defined.)\n- [ ] `grep -rn baseline_advanced_at src/` returns nothing; the stamp is absent from every entry\n      written by `set_baseline`. Tier: **unit** + grep assertion.\n- [ ] A baseline field present-but-null still round-trips as present (guards the `if k in fields`\n      semantics). Tier: **unit** on `set_baseline` + `get_baseline`.\n- [ ] `test_binding_baseline.py:61`'s assertion on the stamp is removed or inverted, and the suite is\n      green. Verify: `make test`.\n- [ ] Marginal packed cost per changed version measured at **<=25% of the 12.79 KB baseline** by the\n      recorded method (rebuild N>=40 consecutive revisions in a clean repo, repack\n      `--window=250 --depth=50`, `(pack_N-pack_1)/(N-1)`). Tier: **repo-level measurement**.\n\n## Files\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/binding_store.py`,\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_inbound_records.py`,\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_binding_baseline.py`\n\nScope: S-M.\n\n## Plan-review remediation (execution corpus and controls)\n\nThe packed-cost corpus is the 40 newest consecutive committed revisions of\n`.bridge_state/bindings.json` from a fresh, **unfiltered** clone of `origin/tickets` taken\nimmediately before A1 implementation. Record the pre-A1 branch tip plus the ordered 40 revision\nSHAs on this ticket before transforming them. For each revision, materialize that exact blob into\nthe same clean scratch-repository harness, commit it, run `git repack -Ad --window=250 --depth=50`,\nand compute `(pack_N - pack_1)/(N-1)`. The before and after measurements must use the same recorded\nSHA list and harness; no live Jira access is required. This makes the 12.79 KB baseline method from\nsession_log `1cb6-d553-6565-49b4` reproducible without relying on an untracked fixture.\n\n`set_baseline` owns the behavior, so its guard covers all three known callers:\n`reconcile._advance_baselines`, the inbound-adopt path, and\n`BindingStore.seed_baselines_from_snapshot`. The seeding caller needs no separate source edit, but\nits unchanged-input behavior is part of the oracle inventory.\n\nAdditional acceptance criteria:\n\n- [ ] Positive control: after a stored baseline value changes from A to B, `set_baseline` persists\n      B and the saved `bindings.json` bytes change; the guard must not degenerate into never-write.\n      Tier: unit on the real `BindingStore` plus its real serialization.\n- [ ] The inbound-adopt path and `seed_baselines_from_snapshot` are inventoried in Phase 4a; any\n      existing coverage is strengthened or a focused test is added if neither proves unchanged\n      input is a no-op and changed input persists.\n- [ ] Source search uses an exact identifier boundary (for example\n      `grep -Rnw baseline_advanced_at src/rebar`) and returns no production reference."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785692328860385001,"uuid":"1994c9ea-9475-4202-96d9-b052a5137c13"}